'<h3>1. Flynn’s taxonomy</h3><p>Computers are classified by how many <b>instruction streams</b> and <b>data streams</b> they process at once:</p>' + K.fig(flynn) +
K.table(['Class', 'Meaning'], [['SISD', 'one processor executes one instruction stream on data in one memory'], ['SIMD', 'one instruction controls many processing elements in lockstep, each on its own data (vector and array processors, GPUs)'], ['MISD', 'one data stream through processors each running different instructions; not commercially built'], ['MIMD', 'many processors run different instruction streams on different data (SMPs, clusters, NUMA)']]) +
'<p>Example: <code>Sum = a[0] + … + a[99]</code> and <code>Mult = b[0] × … × b[99]</code>. SIMD would apply one operation to many elements at once; MIMD could run the sum and the product as two independent programs on two processors.</p>' +
K.table(['Organization', 'Memory'], [['SIMD (distributed memory)', 'one control unit (CU) sends the instruction stream (IS) to processing units PU<sub>1</sub>\u2026PU<sub>n</sub>, each with its own local memory (LM)'], ['MIMD with shared memory', 'each CU/PU pair runs its own instruction stream; all share one memory: <b>big and slower</b>'], ['MIMD with distributed memory', 'each PU has its own local memory and they communicate through an interconnection network: <b>smaller and faster</b>']]) +
'<h3>2. Symmetric multiprocessors (SMP)</h3><p>A stand-alone computer with:</p>' + K.list(['two or more similar processors of comparable capacity;', 'shared main memory and I/O, connected by a bus or other interconnection, with roughly the same memory access time for every processor;', 'shared access to I/O devices (same or different channels);', 'all processors able to perform the same functions (hence "symmetric");', 'one integrated operating system managing the processors, jobs, tasks and files.']) +
K.fig(tight, 'A tightly coupled multiprocessor: processors, I/O and main memory joined by an interconnection network.') + '<p>Multiprogramming interleaves processes on one processor; multiprocessing also overlaps them in time:</p>' + K.fig(tl) +
'<h3>3. Example: scheduling a task graph</h3><p>The lecture illustrates task dependencies and the assignment of tasks to processors with a task graph. A program split into tasks with dependencies is a <b>task graph</b>; assigning its tasks to processors is the scheduler’s job.</p>' +
(G && D ? iod(K.fig(D.taskGraph(G, cp.path), 'The practice problem’s graph; the critical path is highlighted.') + K.fig(D.gantt(two.cores, two.time, ['Core 1', 'Core 2'], cp.path), 'The best two-core schedule: ' + two.time + ' time units against 18 on one core, a speedup of ' + (18 / two.time).toFixed(1) + '.')) : '') +
K.list(['One core: the sum of all task times.', 'n cores: at least the critical path, and at least total work ÷ n.', 'Speedup = one-core time ÷ n-core time.']) +
'<h3>4. The shared bus</h3>' + K.fig(smpOrg, 'SMP organization: each processor has its own L1 and L2 caches on a shared bus to main memory and the I/O subsystem.') + K.table(['Advantages', 'Disadvantages'], [['<b>Simplicity</b>: the simplest multiprocessor organization<br><b>Flexibility</b>: easy to add processors to the bus<br><b>Reliability</b>: the bus is passive, so one failed device does not bring down the system', '<b>Performance</b>: every memory reference crosses the bus, limited by the bus cycle time<br>So each processor gets a <b>cache</b>, which reduces bus traffic but creates the <b>cache coherence</b> problem: if one cache changes a word, copies in other caches become stale. Other processors must be told; this is usually done in hardware.']]) +
'<h3>5. Operating system design considerations</h3>' + K.table(['Issue', 'What the OS must do'], [['Simultaneous processes', 'reentrant OS code so several processors can run it; manage tables to avoid deadlock'], ['Scheduling', 'any processor may schedule, so avoid conflicts; assign ready processes to available processors'], ['Synchronization', 'enforce mutual exclusion and event ordering on shared memory and I/O'], ['Memory management', 'exploit the parallel hardware; coordinate paging across processors'], ['Reliability and fault tolerance', 'degrade gracefully when a processor fails; restructure the schedule']]) +
'<h3>6. Multicore organizations</h3><p>A multicore chip puts several processors (cores) on one die. They differ in how many cache levels each core keeps for itself:</p>' + K.fig(orgs, 'Caches closer to the core are smaller and faster; shared caches let cores share data without going to main memory.') +
'<h3>7. Heterogeneous multicore</h3><p>A chip with <b>more than one kind of core</b>, most often CPUs plus <b>GPUs</b>. GPUs run thousands of parallel threads, so they suit large amounts of vector and matrix data; CPUs are best at serial work. </p>' + K.fig(het, 'Heterogeneous chip elements: CPUs and GPUs with their caches, an on-chip interconnection network, last-level caches and DRAM controllers. Mixing core types raises issues of coordination and correctness.') + '<p><b>Heterogeneous System Architecture (HSA)</b> makes them work together:</p>' +
K.list(['the whole virtual memory space is visible to both CPU and GPU;', 'virtual memory brings pages into physical memory as needed;', 'a coherent memory policy keeps CPU and GPU caches up to date;', 'one programming interface lets programs use both seamlessly.']) +
'<p>The overall objective: let programmers write applications that use the <b>serial power of CPUs</b> and the <b>parallel power of GPUs</b> seamlessly, with efficient coordination at the OS and hardware level.</p>' +
'<h3>8. Key points</h3>' + K.list(['Flynn: SISD, SIMD, MISD, MIMD.', 'SMP: similar processors sharing memory and I/O under one OS.', 'Shared buses are simple but limit performance; caches help but need coherence.', 'Multicore chips differ in which caches are private or shared; heterogeneous chips mix CPUs and GPUs.']);
